Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Who Hid the Blindfold in the Ice?
- Story & Context: Astrid Lind-Vang first rigged her contact-mic’d bone shakers to an overloaded Amiga sampler on the salt-bleached fairgrounds of Tromsø during the 1999 totality. When the main generator sputtered out and soaked the wooden carousel in the reek of spilled kerosene, the wandering eclipse carnival did not panic; Astrid’s troupe doubled their tempo into a frantic 172-BPM skitter while a bowed double-bass wailed like a stranded whale. Amidst the screams and the eerie cold that frosted everyone's eyelashes in seconds, she grabbed a bullhorn and sang "Catch the dark before your teeth go blue," sparking the first riotous outdoor umbral rave.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An eerie, painterly film still of children's carnival blindfolds frozen into crystalline sea-ice beneath an ominous, ring-shaped solar eclipse. A single bullhorn sits steaming on the frost, with faint silhouettes of dancers blurred in motion. Typography rendered in cracked gold carnival paint spells out the lyric across the dark sky.

Track 2: Søren’s Seven-Pencil Gauntlet
- Story & Context: Huddled behind the rotting fish-crates of Narvik harbor, Søren confessed to the troupe elders that he had hollowed out their antique seedpod shakers to hide black-market morphine ampoules during the three-minute blackout of 2015. His fingers, numb inside frayed fingerless woolen mittens smelling of cold seal grease, rattled the illicit gourds against his knuckles at dizzying jungle speeds to mask the tremor of his withdrawal. As the moon slid directly over the blazing solar rim, Søren wept openly into an open ribbon mic, chanting "I drop three stones to watch which one stays warm" over chopped upright bass drones. The resulting recording became the underground confession tape traded between eclipse-chasers across the northern fjords.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up, dramatic chiaroscuro lighting on two bruised, calloused hands in fingerless gray wool mittens tightly shaking a carved wooden gourd. Seven sharpened pencils lie scattered on a salt-crusted harbor dock next to a glowing vacuum-tube microphone, with the eerie violet light of a midday solar eclipse reflecting in cold ocean puddles.

Track 3: Brass Bells Chew the Ash
- Story & Context: Legend across Finnish Lapland insists that when the eclipse of 2026 ended over the iced surface of Lake Inari, the carnival's brass players threw their mutes into the freezing drift and ran, leaving their snares clattering on pure feedback loops. Locals arrived to find only crushed lingonberries stained into the slush, empty painted masks, and a loop pedal still firing phantom 808 sub-bombs beneath the creaking wooden tentpoles. The remaining playback captured a phantom soprano sax harmonizing with a thousand ghost-rattles, whistling the chilling final refrain, "The carousel spins until the shadow rots." To this day, rogue sound systems broadcast the abandoned session into the empty tundra whenever the sky threatens to dim.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A haunting, surrealist post-performance wide shot of an abandoned winter carnival tent pitched on frozen lake ice under a sky returning from black to grey. Brass trumpet mutes and cracked harlequin masks lay half-buried in snow stained crimson with spilled lingonberry juice, while a single speaker cone vibrates sound waves into the falling snow.

